{
  "sn41.4:0.1": "Linked Discourses 41.4 ",
  "sn41.4:0.2": "1. With Citta ",
  "sn41.4:0.3": "Mahaka’s Demonstration ",
  "sn41.4:1.1": "At one time several senior mendicants were staying near Macchikāsaṇḍa in the Wild Mango Grove. ",
  "sn41.4:1.2": "Then Citta the householder went up to them, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to them, ",
  "sn41.4:1.3": "“Sirs, would the senior monks please accept tomorrow’s meal from me in my milkshed?” ",
  "sn41.4:1.4": "They consented with silence. ",
  "sn41.4:1.5": "Then, knowing that the senior mendicants had consented, Citta rose from his seat, bowed, and respectfully circled them, keeping them on his right, before leaving. ",
  "sn41.4:1.6": "Then when the night had passed, the senior mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, went to Citta’s milkshed, and sat down on the seats spread out. ",
  "sn41.4:2.1": "Then Citta served and satisfied the senior mendicants with his own hands with delicious milk-rice made with ghee. ",
  "sn41.4:2.2": "When the senior mendicants had eaten and washed their hands and bowls, they rose from their seats and left. ",
  "sn41.4:2.3": "Citta instructed that the leftovers of the food be distributed, then followed behind the senior mendicants. ",
  "sn41.4:2.4": "Now at that time it was boiling hot. ",
  "sn41.4:2.5": "And those senior mendicants walked along as if their bodies were melting, as happens after a meal. ",
  "sn41.4:3.1": "Now at that time Venerable Mahaka was the most junior me